-- dispatch: a joined constraint instantiates its callee's `where` clause
-- ONCE PER INSTRUCTION it answers, not once for the first
-- (`docs/design/static-dispatch-spike.md` §6.2 Rule U1, §7.2, A.68, A.75).
--
-- A.75 made one constraint answer sites on SEVERAL instructions: nesting
-- two calls of a constrained function over a `let`-bound seed leaves both
-- receivers flex until the inner call's result meets the outer call's
-- parameter, and Rule U1 then joins the two `k.compare` constraints onto
-- one. Resolving that one constraint instantiates the callee — and the
-- callee has a `where` clause of its own, whose slot is numbered by a
-- cursor the INSTRUCTION owns (§7.2). Instantiating it against a single
-- `origin` therefore gave ONE instruction a nested slot and left every
-- other one an argument short: `nestedList` dumped `site 73 0 ext List
-- compare` with no `site 73 1` beside the outer call's `site 76 0` and
-- `site 76 1`, and `Lower.evidenceShapeOk` stopped the build with
-- `internal` — a compiler bug reported about a program whose only fault
-- is that it nests twice over a constrained element.
--
-- Four routes reach it, and the golden shows a CHILD site under each
-- instruction of all four:
--
--   - `nestedList` is the `ext` arm: `k` is `List Int`, whose `compare`
--     is `core/List`'s `pub foreign compare … where a.compare` (§5.2).
--   - `nestedDict` is the same shape through `Dict.insert`, which is
--     where a user meets it — a `Dict` keyed on a list, built by two
--     inserts over a `let`-bound `Dict.empty`. `annotatedDict` beside it
--     is the control, and it is why the `Dict.size` wrapper is there: an
--     annotation pins `k` to `List Int` before the nesting is checked, so
--     each constraint is discharged and marked before any join happens
--     and that form was right all along.
--   - `nestedOwn` is the `top` arm: `Pair` is declared HERE and this
--     module's own `pub compare … where a.compare` answers for it, so the
--     nested slot is instantiated from a local scheme rather than an
--     interface.
--   - `nestedEq` is the same defect under the other well-known name.
--
-- `dispatch/JoinedConstraintSites` is the flat case — one join, no callee
-- of the callee — and `run/NestedConstrainedListKeys` executes all four.
import Dict exposing (Dict)


pub type Box k
    = Empty
    | Box k


pub put : Box k, k -> Box k
    where k.compare : k, k -> Order
put box _ =
    box


pub tag : Box k, k -> Box k
    where k.eq : k, k -> Bool
tag box _ =
    box


pub type Pair a
    = Pair a a


pub compare : Pair a, Pair a -> Order
    where a.compare : a, a -> Order
compare (Pair x _) (Pair y _) =
    x.compare y


pub size : Box k -> Int
size _ =
    1


pub nestedList : Int
nestedList =
    let
        seed =
            Empty
    in
    size (put (put seed [ 1, 2 ]) [ 3 ])


pub nestedDict : Int
nestedDict =
    let
        seed =
            Dict.empty
    in
    Dict.size (Dict.insert (Dict.insert seed [ 1, 2 ] "a") [ 3 ] "b")


pub annotatedDict : Dict (List Int) String
annotatedDict =
    let
        seed =
            Dict.empty
    in
    Dict.insert (Dict.insert seed [ 1, 2 ] "a") [ 3 ] "b"


pub nestedOwn : Int
nestedOwn =
    let
        seed =
            Empty
    in
    size (put (put seed (Pair 1 2)) (Pair 3 4))


pub nestedEq : Int
nestedEq =
    let
        seed =
            Empty
    in
    size (tag (tag seed [ 1, 2 ]) [ 3 ])
